Since this is .NET Core, you can do all of this from the command line. In
PowerShell, running the following command will create an identical
project: dotnet new winforms -n eBookManager.
Our solution needs a class library project to contain the classes that drive the
eBookManager application. In .NET Core 3.0, we have the option of creating either a .NET
Core class library or a .NET Standard class library.
.NET Standard is a bit of a strange concept. In and of itself, it is not a technology, but a
contract; creating a .NET Standard class library simply prevents you from using anything
that is .NET Core — (or framework —) specific, and does not adhere to .NET Standard. The
following document illustrates the .NET Standard versions, and what releases of Core and
Framework support them: https:/ / github. com/ dotnet/ standard/ blob/ master/ docs/
versions. md.
